canvasplayer
------------

Graffiti Markup Language (GML) playback in the browser, on HTML5 <canvas>,
with no dependencies.

Online demo: https://jamiew.github.io/canvasplayer
Code: https://github.com/jamiew/canvasplayer

GML data comes from #000000book, an open database of GML files:
https://000000book.com/data/147.json


Running it locally
------------------

No build step, so any static server works:

    python3 -m http.server 8420

Open http://localhost:8420/?id=161. Also ?latest and ?random. Tags load from
000000book.com over JSONP, so the page needs the network but no backend of its
own. It runs from file:// too. Switching tags in the page keeps whatever mode,
effects and layers you picked.

To debug a tag, turn on the DATA switches. "points" marks every sample and
numbers each stroke where it starts. "vectors" draws an arrow every fourth
sample, longer and brighter the faster the hand was moving. "bounds" draws the
capture screen, the tag's own bounding box and a 0.1 grid. "graph" plots speed
against time.

Tests, which need no browser and no network:

    node test.js


Using it
--------

    <div class="stage"><canvas id="canvas"></canvas></div>
    <script src="gml-player.js"></script>
    <script src="gml-source.js"></script>
    <script>
      GmlSource.load(147, function (tag) {
        new GmlPlayer(document.getElementById('canvas'), tag).play();
      });
    </script>

The canvas sizes itself to its parent, so give that element the dimensions you
want. gml-player.js has no dependencies, touches no DOM but the canvas you hand
it, and does not care where its data came from -- feed it the same shape from
anywhere:

    { id, app, rotate, strokes: [ {points: [[x, y, time], ...]} ] }

gml-source.js is only the #000000book adapter: it fetches a tag and flattens
the GML tree into that. It loads over JSONP because the API answers without an
Access-Control-Allow-Origin header, so fetch cannot read the response.


What it does
------------

Plays a tag in real time from the recorded timestamps. Capture apps write
zeroes, unix epochs, out-of-order samples and minute-long stalls, so the
timeline is repaired first, and what changed is counted on player.timing.

Width follows the speed of the hand: widest where it was slowest, thinnest at
this tag's own peak. Runs of ink start where the line is widest.

Four modes. marker and chisel are handstyles: marker runs a spline through the
samples to take the staircase off a slow hand, and widens as the hand slows.
chisel sweeps a flat nib at a fixed angle, so width comes from direction rather
than speed. hairline and skeleton are the diagrammatic ones.

Four effects. ghost lays the whole tag underneath at low alpha, so you can see
where the hand is going. bleed puts three widening passes under the stroke.
jitter offsets every sample by a fixed amount of noise. fade drops the alpha of
older ink, leaving a comet tail behind the hand.

It opens in marker, with ghost and the drips on.

Runs come from ranking each stroke's own samples slowest-first and taking a few
of them, spaced apart. Ranking within a stroke rather than against an absolute
speed is what makes them land on a fast tag and a slow one alike.

One pool feeds both how fat a run is and how far it falls: how slowly the pen
was moving where it started, and how long it sat there. Runs fall straight down.
They may lean as they go, but only by a fraction of how far they have already
fallen.

Effects reach the runs as well as the stroke, since a run is ink too: fade ages
them, jitter moves them with the stroke they hang off, bleed soaks them
outwards. ghost is the exception, and lays down the ink alone.

Playback is monochrome in both light and dark, so the overlays separate
themselves by weight, dashes and alpha rather than colour.


